@namespace Bit.BlazorUI.Demo.Client.Core.Pages.Components.Extras.Chart

<DemoExample Title="Grouped" RazorCode="@groupedRazorCode" CsharpCode="@groupedCsharpCode" Id="bar-grouped">
    <div class="example-desc">
        The default bar layout: every dataset gets its own slot inside each category, so the series stand side by
        side and can be compared at a glance. <code>CategoryPercentage</code> controls how much of the band the
        whole group takes and <code>BarPercentage</code> how much of its slot each bar fills.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="BitChartSampleData.Revenue()" Options="_bottom" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Stacked" RazorCode="@stackedRazorCode" CsharpCode="@stackedCsharpCode" Id="bar-stacked">
    <div class="example-desc">
        Setting <code>Stacked</code> on the value scale makes the datasets accumulate instead of sitting side by
        side, so each bar reads as a total broken into parts. Positive and negative values stack away from zero
        independently.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="BitChartSampleData.Revenue()" Options="@_stacked" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Horizontal" RazorCode="@horizontalRazorCode" CsharpCode="@horizontalCsharpCode" Id="bar-horizontal">
    <div class="example-desc">
        <code>IndexAxis = BitChartIndexAxis.Y</code> turns the chart on its side. Horizontal bars give long
        category names room to breathe, so they suit rankings and survey results.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="@PerColor()" Options="@_horizontal" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Rounded + per-bar colors" RazorCode="@roundedRazorCode" CsharpCode="@roundedCsharpCode" Id="bar-rounded" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div class="example-desc">
        <code>BorderRadius</code> rounds the corners away from the baseline, so a bar keeps a flat foot on the
        axis. A list in <code>BackgroundColors</code> gives one color per category instead of per series.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="@PerColorRounded()" Options="@_rounded" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Floating bars" RazorCode="@floatingRazorCode" CsharpCode="@floatingCsharpCode" Id="bar-floating">
    <div class="example-desc">
        With <code>RangeData</code> each bar spans a <code>[low, high]</code> pair rather than growing from zero
        &mdash; the shape for temperature ranges, price bands and Gantt-style schedules.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="@Floating()" Options="_noLegend" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="100% stacked" RazorCode="@stacked100RazorCode" CsharpCode="@stacked100CsharpCode" Id="bar-stacked-100">
    <div class="example-desc">
        Adding <code>Stacked100</code> normalizes every category to 100%, which shifts the story from totals to
        composition. The tooltip still shows the original values.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="BitChartSampleData.Revenue()" Options="@_stacked100" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Pattern fills" RazorCode="@patternedRazorCode" CsharpCode="@patternedCsharpCode" Id="bar-patterned">
    <div class="example-desc">
        <code>BackgroundPattern</code> fills the bars with a repeating hatch, grid or dot texture. Patterns keep
        the series distinguishable when the chart is printed in black and white or read by someone with a color
        vision deficiency.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="@Patterned()" Options="_bottom" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Top-rounded corners" RazorCode="@topRoundedRazorCode" CsharpCode="@topRoundedCsharpCode" Id="bar-top-rounded" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div class="example-desc">
        <code>BorderRadiusCorners</code> sets each corner separately when the automatic choice is not what you
        want &mdash; here only the two top corners, with <code>BitChartBorderRadiusCorners.Top</code>.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="@TopRounded()" Options="_noLegend" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Auto-rotated labels" RazorCode="@rotatedRazorCode" CsharpCode="@rotatedCsharpCode" Id="bar-rotated" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div class="example-desc">
        Category labels that do not fit their band tilt automatically, just far enough to fit and never past
        <code>MaxRotation</code>. The axis then reserves the extra height the rotated text needs.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="BitChartSampleData.Countries()" Options="_noLegend" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Stacked groups" RazorCode="@stackedGroupsRazorCode" CsharpCode="@stackedGroupsCsharpCode" Id="bar-stacked-groups">
    <div class="example-desc">
        Datasets sharing a <code>Stack</code> id accumulate together, and each id gets its own column. Two stacks
        side by side compare composition across scenarios &mdash; and the value axis fits the tallest stack, not
        the sum of them all.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="@StackedGroups()" Options="@_stackedGroups" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Minimum length and a custom base" RazorCode="@minLengthRazorCode" CsharpCode="@minLengthCsharpCode" Id="bar-min-length">
    <div class="example-desc">
        <code>MinBarLength</code> keeps near-zero values visible instead of collapsing them into the axis line,
        and <code>Base</code> moves the value bars grow from &mdash; here a target of 100, so the chart reads as
        distance from target rather than absolute size.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="@AgainstTarget()" Options="@_target" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Overlaid series and skipped gaps" RazorCode="@overlayRazorCode" CsharpCode="@overlayCsharpCode" Id="bar-overlay">
    <div class="example-desc">
        A dataset with <code>Grouped = false</code> leaves the grouping layout and keeps the full category band,
        which is how a target or budget series is drawn behind the actuals. <code>SkipNull</code> then lets the
        remaining bars widen over the categories where a series has no value, instead of leaving a hole.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="@Overlay()" Options="_bottom" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Error bars" RazorCode="@errorBarsRazorCode" CsharpCode="@errorBarsCsharpCode" Id="bar-error-bars">
    <div class="example-desc">
        <code>ErrorData</code> hangs an uncertainty interval off each value and draws it as a capped whisker through
        the bar's tip &mdash; a confidence band, a measurement tolerance, a spread. A <code>BitChartErrorBar</code> is
        built from one number for a symmetric interval or two for an asymmetric one, a <code>null</code> entry leaves
        that value bare, and the interval is named in the tooltip so it is read as well as seen.
        <code>ErrorBarColor</code>, <code>ErrorBarWidth</code> and <code>ErrorBarCapWidth</code> style it; lines and
        scatter points take the same property.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="@Measured()" Options="_bottom" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Waterfall" RazorCode="@waterfallRazorCode" CsharpCode="@waterfallCsharpCode" Id="bar-waterfall">
    <div class="example-desc">
        A waterfall shows how a starting figure becomes an ending one, and it needs nothing the chart does not
        already have: <code>RangeData</code> floats each bar between the running total before and after the step,
        and <code>BackgroundColorFn</code> colors it by direction &mdash; green for a gain, red for a loss, neutral
        for the totals that sit on the axis. A <code>Callbacks.Label</code> then reports the step rather than the
        two ends of the bar.
    </div>
    <div class="chart-wrapper"><div class="chart-container"><BitChart RespectReducedMotion="false" Type="BitChartType.Bar" Data="@Waterfall()" Options="_waterfall" /></div></div>
</DemoExample>
